<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>LPA GO Dialogue Script</title><!--[if gte mso 9]><xml><w:WordDocument><w:View>Print</w:View></w:WordDocument></xml><![endif]--><style>body{font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.35}h1{font-size:22pt;font-weight:normal}h2{font-size:16pt;font-weight:normal;border-bottom:1px solid #b68235}h3{font-size:12pt;color:#7a5520;margin-top:14pt}.m{color:#666;font-size:10pt}.o{margin:8pt 0 2pt 0}.r{margin:0 0 2pt 24pt}</style></head><body><h1>LPA GO! — Dialogue Script</h1><p class=m>Exported from lpa-go-script.js, 2026-09-28. Grades: Convince / Neutral / Backfire. Keep the bracketed IDs so edits can be mapped back into the game.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:auto">1. Active Andy [andy]</h2><p class=m>Andy, 23 · Gym instructor, just ORD</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>LPA is for old people.</li><li>I've got nothing worth protecting.</li><li>My mum will just handle everything.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Sports Hall gym · Morning</h3><p><b>Andy:</b> Lisa! Wah, LPA again? Bro, I just ORD. My knees still got another sixty years. This one is for my ah ma, not me.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-02, C-05):</b> Fair, most people your age think so. But an LPA is about capacity, not age. Anyone 21 and up can make one, and capacity can be lost for weeks after a crash. You ride every day.</p><p class=r><b>Andy replies:</b> …Okay, the crash part I don't like to think about. Say more later, I still got three laps.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Actually one in four Singaporeans will be 65 and above by 2030, so it's everyone's problem.</p><p class=r><b>Andy replies:</b> Uh huh. Come back when it's my problem, coach.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> You never know, bro, if you crash and end up brain-dead, at least your mum can pull the plug.</p><p class=r><b>Andy replies:</b> Eh, that's dark, and that's not even what I want. Go away lah.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Myth: donees cannot decide on life-sustaining treatment, MCA s 13(8)</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Sports Hall gym · Midday</h3><p><b>Andy:</b> Say I do lose capacity for a while. So what? I got no house, no shares. Bank got maybe two thousand. Nothing to protect.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-09, C-08):</b> Two thousand isn't the issue. Access is. In ICU, nobody can touch that account, cancel your phone plan or claim your motor insurance without legal authority. A donee can, and a personal-welfare donee decides where you go on discharge.</p><p class=r><b>Andy replies:</b> Discharge… I didn't think about that. My mum would want me home. OK.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> You can choose to make the LPA for personal welfare only, or property only, or both.</p><p class=r><b>Andy replies:</b> Right. So I can choose nothing also.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Doesn't matter. The government takes over your account anyway if you don't have one.</p><p class=r><b>Andy replies:</b> That's not a thing. You're making stuff up.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False; nobody "takes over", a court must appoint a deputy on application (C-21)</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — Ketapang Boutique · Evening</h3><p><b>Andy:</b> Honestly my mum will just settle everything. She's my next-of-kin, right? Next-of-kin can decide.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-21, C-26):</b> 'Next-of-kin' sounds official, but it gives your mum no legal authority. OPG's brochure lists that as myth number one. Without an LPA she applies to court to be your deputy. With one, she's your donee the day it's needed.</p><p class=r><b>Andy replies:</b> So I'm not doing it for me. I'm doing it so she doesn't have to do court. …Give me the flyer.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> The court would probably appoint her as deputy in the end anyway.</p><p class=r><b>Andy replies:</b> Then it's fine, right? Later lah.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> If you don't sign, your mum can't even visit you in hospital.</p><p class=r><b>Andy replies:</b> Bro. Don't scare people.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False and manipulative</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Andy runs (literally) to the CC noticeboard, pins his name, then adds his mother's: "She better do one too."</p><p><b>Legacy line (Andy):</b> I appointed my mum and my sister, jointly and severally, so whoever picks up first can act. Learned that from a chilli-growing auntie. Mum accepted on her phone before I'd even sat down with the doctor.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">2. Studious Stanley [stanley]</h2><p class=m>Stanley, 26 · Law graduate, training contract</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>I'll do it when I actually have assets.</li><li>If I'm the donee for my parents, I obviously understand it; no urgency for mine.</li><li>The forms are trivial; I'll do it myself one weekend.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Library · Morning</h3><p><b>Stanley:</b> Lisa, I teach my parents about this. Section 11, section 12, Form 1, Form 2. I'll execute mine once I've got something in my name. No estate, no urgency.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-08, C-05):</b> You're thinking like a property lawyer. A personal-welfare LPA has nothing to do with your estate: where you live, who cares for you, who consents to treatment. Your capacity is the asset, and you can't sign it over after you lose it.</p><p class=r><b>Stanley replies:</b> …Section 11(1)(a). Personal welfare. Fine, that lands. Don't look so pleased.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> There's a 2022 article arguing the Banks v Goodfellow test for wills should be recast using the MCA's section 5 abilities, it shows how central section 5 is to everything.</p><p class=r><b>Stanley replies:</b> I've read it. Twice. What's your point about me?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Once you're a lawyer you can just certify your own LPA and skip the queue.</p><p class=r><b>Stanley replies:</b> You cannot certify yourself, Lisa. That's... no. Come back when you've read the Regulations.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: a certificate issuer cannot be the donor, the donor's family member or donee, Regs reg 7(2)</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Lee &amp; Lim LLC · Midday</h3><p><b>Stanley:</b> My parents named me as their donee, jointly with my sister. I'm literally in the system. If anything happens to me, everyone in this family knows what to do.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-07, C-19):</b> If your parents appointed you jointly, the whole thing stalls when you can't act. Losing capacity ends your appointment, and without a replacement donee they're back to court. Your LPA and theirs are linked. Making yours is part of being a good donee.</p><p class=r><b>Stanley replies:</b> Section 12(5), default is joint. Section 15(5)(d). I should have told them jointly and severally with a replacement. …I'm going to fix that tonight. And mine.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> As a donee you'll have to keep records, the Public Guardian can ask you for information any time.</p><p class=r><b>Stanley replies:</b> I know. Regulation 38. Was there a question?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> If you get sick your sister automatically takes over your donee role, so it's fine.</p><p class=r><b>Stanley replies:</b> A donee can't appoint a substitute. Section 12(8)(a). You're at a law firm, Lisa. Do better.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: no automatic substitution; a donee cannot pass authority to someone else, MCA s 12(8)</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — Kopitiam · Evening</h3><p><b>Stanley:</b> Fine. But I don't need your roadshow. Form 1 is a tick-box exercise. I'll do it on OPGO one weekend.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-17, C-18):</b> You still need a certificate issuer to meet you in person, and it can't be you, your family or your donee. The CI can't open your draft until every donee has accepted on OPGO. Dr Cheryl and Lawyer Lee are both at the CC on Sunday. Book it, and bring your parents for the replacement-donee fix.</p><p class=r><b>Stanley replies:</b> …Efficient. Fine. I'll bring my parents. And my sister. Don't tell Lawyer Lee I got schooled by a social work student.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Since April 2026 the Form 1 application is free for citizens, so cost isn't your excuse.</p><p class=r><b>Stanley replies:</b> Cost was never my excuse. Time is.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Doing it on OPGO is basically instant, it's registered the moment you click submit.</p><p class=r><b>Stanley replies:</b> Three-week waiting period, Lisa. It's on page 20 of the OPG guide. I'm going home.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: the CI submits after certification, then OPG holds a mandatory 3-week waiting period, Guide D2</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Stanley walks his parents to the CC noticeboard and explains "jointly and severally" to them with a napkin diagram.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Stanley):</b> Next year I'll be certifying these. Better to have made one first.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">3. Delivering Dinesh [dinesh]</h2><p class=m>Dinesh, 29 · Food-delivery rider</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>No time, every minute I stop I lose money.</li><li>I got no assets, an LPA is for people with property.</li><li>If something happens, my brother will sort it out.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Hawker Centre pick-up counter · Morning</h3><p><b>Dinesh:</b> Lisa, I've got four orders stacking. Whatever it is, sixty seconds.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-18):</b> Sixty seconds: Sunday, CC, free, doctor and lawyer on site, your brother taps 'accept' on his phone, volunteers do the online part with you. Total time: the one thing you can't buy back if you're hit on the PIE.</p><p class=r><b>Dinesh replies:</b> …That's fifty-five seconds. Respect. Find me tonight at the void deck, I'll listen properly.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Did you know the LPA was introduced in 2010 and about 400,000 citizens have made one?</p><p class=r><b>Dinesh replies:</b> Good for them. Order's up, bye.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> You riders are the highest-risk group, statistically you'll be in an accident soon.</p><p class=r><b>Dinesh replies:</b> Don't curse me before my shift, bro.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Fabricated statistic, tactless</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Blk 5 void deck · Night</h3><p><b>Dinesh:</b> OK, properly. I rent a room. I don't own anything. LPA is a rich-people document, no?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-09, C-21):</b> It's a cash-flow document for you. A month in hospital and your parents' money, the rent and the accident claim all stall. A property-and-affairs donee can run your account and receive the payout. Nobody else legally can, not even your brother.</p><p class=r><b>Dinesh replies:</b> My amma would be calling my phone for weeks. …OK. Who can be the donee, my brother is 24, no income, is that allowed?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> The LPA also covers personal welfare, like where you'd live if you needed care.</p><p class=r><b>Dinesh replies:</b> I live in a rented room, Lisa. Where else would I live.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> You should appoint a professional donee, they'll manage your money better than your brother.</p><p class=r><b>Dinesh replies:</b> Pay someone to manage my two thousand dollars? Are you selling something?</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Misread: professional donees are for people with no suitable family or friends, and they charge fees, C-22</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — Hawker Centre pick-up counter · Evening (rain outside)</h3><p><b>Dinesh:</b> I told my brother. He says: 'Don't worry, if anything I'll just handle.' See? Sorted.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-26, C-10, C-28):</b> He means it, and he can't. The bank won't accept 'just handle'. Without an LPA he applies to court for deputyship: filing, a doctor's report, fees, waiting. With one, once a doctor certifies you can't decide, he declares it on OPGO and sends the bank your LPA the same day.</p><p class=r><b>Dinesh replies:</b> Send the bank the LPA. That's all he'd have to do? …Give me two flyers. He's coming too.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> You could write down your wishes so he knows what you want, the law says a donee must consider any written statement you made.</p><p class=r><b>Dinesh replies:</b> He knows what I want. Send money home, that's all.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Brothers have automatic rights as next-of-kin, so actually he's right, you just need it as backup.</p><p class=r><b>Dinesh replies:</b> So why am I talking to you in the rain?</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: The myth itself (C-21)</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Dinesh's e-bike gets a small yellow "Legacy Day Sunday" flag; his route now passes the CC.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Dinesh):</b> Brother's my donee, jointly and severally with my cousin in case he's riding too.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">4. Hustling Hafiz [hafiz]</h2><p class=m>Hafiz, 34 · Hawker, mee rebus &amp; nasi lemak</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>My wife can handle it, husband and wife, same thing.</li><li>I already have a wasiat and my faraid is settled; that's my planning done.</li><li>If I sign this, the bank will let her take over now, I'm not ready for that.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Stall · Morning</h3><p><b>Hafiz:</b> Lisa, take your mee. LPA? Bro, my wife and I share everything. If I'm down, she runs the stall. Husband and wife, same thing what.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-21, C-09):</b> In the kitchen, same thing. At the bank, not. Being a spouse gives her no authority over your account, the NEA licence or your supplier accounts. Without an LPA the stall stops until she gets a court order. As your donee, she just carries on.</p><p class=r><b>Hafiz replies:</b> The licence is in my name. The supplier account is in my name. …Eh. Sambal's burning. Come find me at the market later.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> You should think about what happens to the stall if you can't work for a long time.</p><p class=r><b>Hafiz replies:</b> I think about it every time my back hurts. Doesn't help. Next!</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Sign the LPA and Sarah can start running the business side right away, take some load off you.</p><p class=r><b>Hafiz replies:</b> So she takes over while I'm still here? That's not what I want. Number 42!</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: the donee can act only when the donor lacks capacity, MCA s 13(1)</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Minimart · Evening</h3><p><b>Hafiz:</b> Already got my planning. Made a wasiat with a lawyer last year, faraid all clear for the kids. I'm more prepared than most people my age.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-25):</b> You are, for after. A wasiat and faraid speak when you've passed. The LPA is for the years you might be alive but unable to decide. It's revoked the moment you pass, so it never touches your faraid.</p><p class=r><b>Hafiz replies:</b> Alive but cannot decide. That's the gap. …My father was like that for two years. I never thought about it that way.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Wills use a different capacity test from the LPA, an old English case called Banks v Goodfellow.</p><p class=r><b>Hafiz replies:</b> OK… and my chillies?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> An LPA replaces your will, so you can throw the wasiat away.</p><p class=r><b>Hafiz replies:</b> Throw away my wasiat? Astaghfirullah. No.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: a donee cannot make a will for the donor, and the LPA ends at death, MCA s 13(9)(c)</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — Stall closing · Night</h3><p><b>Hafiz:</b> OK. Last thing. Sarah. If I put her name, and I'm out for one month, she needs to pay suppliers, pay rent. But what if the bank says no to her?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-10, C-28, C-07):</b> Two protections. The LPA only wakes up when a doctor certifies you lack capacity and Sarah declares it on OPGO; until then you're in charge. And you can add your brother as a second donee, jointly and severally, so either can pay the fishmonger.</p><p class=r><b>Hafiz replies:</b> Sarah and my brother, either one can act. And nothing happens unless I really cannot, and a doctor says so. …Sunday, right? Sarah's coming too. She has a stall in her name also.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Your donee has a legal duty to act in your best interests and can be reported to the Public Guardian if she doesn't.</p><p class=r><b>Hafiz replies:</b> Report my wife? Lisa, please.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Once she's the donee, the money is basically hers to manage as she likes.</p><p class=r><b>Hafiz replies:</b> That's not how I want anyone to think about my money, not even Sarah.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: best interests, preservation of property, gift limits, separate accounts, s 6(7), s 14</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Hafiz pins two names. His stall gets a "Legacy Day: we'll be closed Sunday morning!" sign.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Hafiz):</b> Sarah and my brother, jointly and severally. And I ticked the box that they need the court's approval to deal with the flat, typed in our address, not because I don't trust them. Because it's the kids' home.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">5. Multitasking Mei Ling [meiLing]</h2><p class=m>Mei Ling, 38 · HR executive, mother of two</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>We've talked about it as a family; everyone knows the plan.</li><li>I have zero time; this is one more form.</li><li>My parents are the ones who need it, not me.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Minimart · Morning (before work)</h3><p><b>Mei Ling:</b> Lisa, hi, hello, yes. We discussed all this at Chinese New Year. Everyone knows: if anything happens, my husband decides for me, I decide for my parents. Talking is done.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-26, C-21):</b> Talking is the part the bank can't see. A family agreement gives nobody legal authority; the law only recognises the donee in a registered LPA. One in three people in the national study said what you just said. That's why families end up in court anyway.</p><p class=r><b>Mei Ling replies:</b> …So we agreed on a plan the bank will ignore. That's very us. Talk later, I'm late.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Written wishes matter: a donee must consider any written statement you made while you had capacity.</p><p class=r><b>Mei Ling replies:</b> I'll write it down. When I have a second. Which is never.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Family agreements are legally binding if there were witnesses at the dinner, so you're probably fine.</p><p class=r><b>Mei Ling replies:</b> That is definitely not true and now I don't trust anything you say.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Library · Evening (laptop open)</h3><p><b>Mei Ling:</b> I have eleven minutes before bath time. An LPA is a form, a lawyer, an appointment, a fee, I cannot add one more thing this year.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-16, C-18, C-10):</b> Then let the roadshow carry it. Form 1 is tick-boxes on OPGO with Singpass, your husband accepts on his phone, the certificate issuer is in the next room, and the citizen fee is zero. One Sunday hour, then it sleeps until needed. Bring your parents, same hour.</p><p class=r><b>Mei Ling replies:</b> One hour, all four of us, done. …That's actually less time than the dinner where we 'discussed' it.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> You could use Form 2 to customise the powers exactly the way your family wants.</p><p class=r><b>Mei Ling replies:</b> A lawyer. More time. No.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> You don't even need to go, just fill it up online and it registers itself, no need to see anyone.</p><p class=r><b>Mei Ling replies:</b> If it were that easy I'd have done it. Bye.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: a certificate issuer must certify the donor in person, Regs reg 7; Guide D1 step 2</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — Parents' flat · Night</h3><p><b>Mei Ling:</b> Look at them. They're the ones who need it. I'm 38. If I go before them the universe is broken anyway.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-07, C-08):</b> You're their plan, Mei Ling. If you're the one in hospital, who does the pill box, who pays their bills? Your LPA lets your husband and your sister, jointly and severally, step in without a court application. Protecting you is protecting them.</p><p class=r><b>Mei Ling replies:</b> …If I'm in hospital, who does this box. Nobody. OK. Me, husband, my sister as replacement. And I'm doing my parents' with Dr Cheryl on Sunday.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Your parents should make theirs soon, while their capacity is clearly intact, after it goes it's too late.</p><p class=r><b>Mei Ling replies:</b> I know. That's what I said. Them, not me.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> You can make one LPA that covers you and your parents together, saves time.</p><p class=r><b>Mei Ling replies:</b> One form for three people? That's not how any form works.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: each donor makes their own instrument</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Mei Ling's parents appear beside her at the CC noticeboard the next morning; her mother pins with a shaky hand.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Mei Ling):</b> Four LPAs in one hour. My husband, my sister as replacement, and I'm my parents' donee, with my sister, jointly and severally.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">6. Nurturing Nadia [nadia]</h2><p class=m>Nadia, 41 · CC front-desk officer, mother of Danial</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>Danial is the priority; I can't think about myself.</li><li>If I appoint Rizal, what happens to Danial's money and care, can my donee handle that?</li><li>The court process for Danial is already a mountain; I can't do two things.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — CC front desk · Midday</h3><p><b>Nadia:</b> Lisa, I know the roadshow, I'm the one pinning it. But I've got the deputyship application for Danial in my bag. That's my mountain. My own LPA can wait ten years.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-05, C-21):</b> It can't wait, because you're Danial's whole system. If you lose capacity, Rizal needs authority over your accounts and your care on top of Danial's. With your LPA he has one problem instead of two. Yours is part of Danial's plan.</p><p class=r><b>Nadia replies:</b> …One problem instead of two. Nobody's put it like that. Find me at the pavilion, Danial likes company.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Deputyship for Danial before he turns 21 is possible, the court can act early if he's likely to still lack capacity, and parents are preferred.</p><p class=r><b>Nadia replies:</b> Yes. Section 21. I've read it more times than you have.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> You can just add Danial as a person in your own LPA so it covers him too.</p><p class=r><b>Nadia replies:</b> That's... no. Please don't tell other parents that.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: an LPA covers the donor only; Danial's decisions need deputyship</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Pavilion · Evening</h3><p><b>Nadia:</b> Suppose I appoint Rizal. He's good. But my salary pays for Danial's therapy. If I'm gone, not dead, just gone, can Rizal use my money for Danial? Or does the law lock it up?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-14, C-13, C-16):</b> A property-and-affairs donee pays the household bills but must preserve your property for your own upkeep first. Spending on Danial beyond that may count as a gift, so on Form 1 tick that gifts are allowed, state the amount, and write why. For exact wording, that's Form 2 and Lawyer Lee.</p><p class=r><b>Nadia replies:</b> Tick the gift box, state the amount, write the reason. And for Danial's own money, the trust thing?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Your donee has to act in your best interests, considering your beliefs and values.</p><p class=r><b>Nadia replies:</b> My values are 'keep Danial in therapy'. Will the bank know that?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Your donee can spend your money however the family needs, it's family money once the LPA kicks in.</p><p class=r><b>Nadia replies:</b> If that were true, I'd be more worried, not less.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: s 6(7) and s 14 limits; DGuide C8</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — Day Activity Centre gate · Night</h3><p><b>Nadia:</b> I believe you. But Lisa, look at this file. Affidavits, a doctor's report, the court fee. I cannot start a second process.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-18, C-16):</b> You're not starting a second process, you're skipping one. Form 1 online, Rizal accepts on his phone, Dr Cheryl certifies, submitted before you leave the CC. The court file in your arm is what your family would face for you without one.</p><p class=r><b>Nadia replies:</b> The short route. For me. …OK. Rizal and my sister, jointly and severally. Aisyah as replacement when she's 21, I'll redo it then. Give me the flyer before I change my mind.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> The court prefers to keep deputies' powers limited and supervised, so Danial's order will be carefully scoped.</p><p class=r><b>Nadia replies:</b> I know. It's still a mountain.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Honestly, the deputyship for Danial isn't needed if you make your LPA, your donee's authority extends to him.</p><p class=r><b>Nadia replies:</b> Stop. That is exactly the kind of thing that gets families in trouble.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Nadia pins her name; Danial pins his koi drawing next to it.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Nadia):</b> Mine's done. Danial's deputyship hearing is next month, but at least if I go down, Rizal only has one file, not two.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">7. Single Suzanne [suzanne]</h2><p class=m>Suzanne, 44 · Freelance photographer</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>I have nobody to appoint. End of story.</li><li>My brother is in Australia, pointless.</li><li>A paid stranger managing my money? That's how people get robbed.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Ketapang Boutique shoot · Morning</h3><p><b>Suzanne:</b> Lisa, I've read about LPAs. They assume you've got a spouse and three filial children. I've got a cat and a brother in Perth. There is nobody to appoint. Next.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-22, C-30):</b> That's the exact gap the Professional Donees scheme fixed. With no family or friend, you can appoint a professional donee registered with OPG for your money, or a licensed trust company for property. A trusted friend can be your donee for personal welfare. 'Nobody' is one in six people.</p><p class=r><b>Suzanne replies:</b> A registered professional. Not a random uncle. …Higher with the reflector. And come to the kopitiam at noon.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> The trend is real, more singles and childless couples; the researchers say Singapore's family-first model has to evolve.</p><p class=r><b>Suzanne replies:</b> So I'm a trend. Great. Still nobody.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Just put your brother down, distance doesn't matter, everything's online now.</p><p class=r><b>Suzanne replies:</b> He can barely video-call. No.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Over-claim; a donee in Perth can't practically deal with a Singapore bank or hospital alone</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Kopitiam · Midday</h3><p><b>Suzanne:</b> OK, say my brother wants to be donee. He's eight hours' flight away. If I'm in a coma, he's useless for the first two weeks.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-07, C-35):</b> Then don't make him act alone. Name your brother and a Singapore-based friend jointly and severally: either can act, so your friend handles the first two weeks and your brother flies in. Add a replacement donee. Form 1 is built for exactly this.</p><p class=r><b>Suzanne replies:</b> Either one can act. Friend here, brother later. …Mei, my assistant from the wedding gigs. She'd do it.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> A donee's appointment ends if they lose capacity or go bankrupt, so choose carefully.</p><p class=r><b>Suzanne replies:</b> Noted. Still eight hours away.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Appoint your brother jointly with your friend, that way they have to agree on everything, safest option.</p><p class=r><b>Suzanne replies:</b> So my friend can't do anything until Perth wakes up. That's worse.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Misleading: jointly means both must act together, s 12(4)–(5)</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — School hall shoot · Evening</h3><p><b>Suzanne:</b> Last thing. A paid professional with access to my savings. I've photographed enough family lawsuits to know how that ends.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-20, C-14, C-15):</b> A professional donee is the most watched person in the system: registered with OPG, fee agreed in writing, an annual report to OPG, and the court can suspend their powers if your assets are at risk. You also set the limits: no gifts, no dealing with your unit without court approval. They manage; they don't own.</p><p class=r><b>Suzanne replies:</b> No gifts, no sale without a court. An annual report. …Fine. Mei for welfare, professional for the money, brother as replacement. Get in the shot, Lisa, the light's going.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> The 2016 amendments were partly a response to a high-profile abuse case, so the safeguards are recent and strict.</p><p class=r><b>Suzanne replies:</b> Recent. Strict. Words.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Professionals are bonded and insured, so if they steal, you're fully refunded.</p><p class=r><b>Suzanne replies:</b> You don't actually know that, do you.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Over-claim: security bonds are not standard practice in Singapore</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Suzanne's Legacy Day photo becomes the game's ending screen, the whole cast at the CC.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Suzanne):</b> Turns out 'nobody' was a failure of imagination. And of reading Part C of the guide.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">8. Driving Desmond [desmond]</h2><p class=m>Desmond, 47 · Taxi driver, 12-hour shifts</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>I'm the one who handles everything, if I'm down, that's what the insurance is for.</li><li>I don't want anyone selling my flat behind my back.</li><li>Lina doesn't know the accounts; putting her in charge is asking for trouble.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Kopitiam · Morning</h3><p><b>Desmond:</b> Lisa, I've got a two-hundred-thousand life policy. Anything happens, my family is covered. LPA is for people with no insurance.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-27, C-09):</b> Insurance pays if you die. The LPA covers a stroke that leaves you alive and unable to decide for six months. Someone still has to pay the instalment, school fees and electricity from your account. Your policy can't. Your donee can.</p><p class=r><b>Desmond replies:</b> …Alive and cannot decide. My mother-in-law was like that. Lina's brother had to go court. I forgot that part.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> About 400,000 citizens have made one, and Form 1 is free for citizens now.</p><p class=r><b>Desmond replies:</b> Free, free. Everything free also I got no time. Shift starts.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Your donee can also change your insurance nomination if the family situation changes, so it's more flexible than the policy alone.</p><p class=r><b>Desmond replies:</b> Change my nomination? Over my dead body. Literally.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: donees cannot make or revoke insurance nominations, MCA s 13(9)</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Minimart · Midday (between shifts)</h3><p><b>Desmond:</b> What I really don't like: sign this, then someone gets to sell my flat. I've seen it. Family fights, flat gone.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-15, C-13):</b> You control that on the form. Form 1 asks whether your donee needs the court's approval to sell, transfer or mortgage your property. Tick yes and type in this flat's address, and it only moves with a judge looking. And by law your donee must preserve your property for your maintenance.</p><p class=r><b>Desmond replies:</b> A judge has to look, and it's my address on the form. OK. That's the box I want.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> The Public Guardian can investigate complaints about a donee.</p><p class=r><b>Desmond replies:</b> After the flat is sold? Too late.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Don't worry, an LPA never lets anyone sell property, that's just not possible.</p><p class=r><b>Desmond replies:</b> My cousin's donee sold his shophouse. Don't bluff me.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: a property-and-affairs donee can buy, sell, rent and mortgage property unless restricted, Guide B4</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — Town Council office · Evening</h3><p><b>Desmond:</b> Lina. She's never paid a bill online in her life. Make her donee and she'll be lost. Make my brother donee and Lina will feel I don't trust her.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-07, C-08, C-13, C-33):</b> Do both, jointly and severally: Lina for personal welfare, because she knows you; Lina and your brother for property, so either can pay bills and he can show her the ropes. Write a one-page 'how the accounts work' tonight. Once registered, tell the bank and CPF Board and ask what they'll need from Lina.</p><p class=r><b>Desmond replies:</b> Lina for me, Lina-plus-brother for money. A cheat sheet, and I tell the bank first. …Alright. Sunday. Book me. Book Lina too, she's got her own name on the CPF, not mine.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Your donee needs to be 21 and above and not bankrupt, your brother qualifies.</p><p class=r><b>Desmond replies:</b> I know he qualifies. I asked about Lina.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Appoint your brother; you can always keep it secret from Lina.</p><p class=r><b>Desmond replies:</b> Keep secrets from my wife on your advice? Go home, Lisa.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Bad advice; OPG guidance encourages telling family and the institutions, Guide D3</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> The taxi gets a rooftop "Legacy Day Sunday" ad panel and drives the loop as a moving advertisement.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Desmond):</b> I ticked the flat box. Lina wrote the cheat sheet with me. She says my handwriting is the real incapacity.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">9. Caregiving Carol [carol]</h2><p class=m>Carol, 49 · Full-time caregiver to her mother</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>I'm the healthy one; my LPA is the last thing on the list.</li><li>Even with an LPA, family still ends up in court, look at us.</li><li>I'm terrified of making the wrong decisions for Mum; why would I give someone that burden for me?</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Lee &amp; Lim LLC waiting room · Morning</h3><p><b>Carol:</b> Lisa, love, I'd sell your roadshow door to door. But my own LPA? I'm the one who's fine. Mum's the file, not me.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-21, C-05):</b> Everything runs through you, which makes you the single point of failure. If you were in hospital next week, Marcus would need authority for you, and nobody would have any for Mum. Yours takes an hour and spares them a second court file. You know what that file looks like.</p><p class=r><b>Carol replies:</b> A second file. God. …Fine, keep talking, but not here, Mum gets upset when I cry.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Your mother's case shows why capacity has to exist at the time of signing, after that it's too late.</p><p class=r><b>Carol replies:</b> Yes, Lisa. I know.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Once you're appointed Mum's deputy, you're covered too, it works both ways.</p><p class=r><b>Carol replies:</b> It does not work both ways and I'm too tired to explain why.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: a deputyship for Mum gives Carol no protection for herself</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Kampung jetty · Midday</h3><p><b>Carol:</b> This is the report for the court. Sixteen weeks since we started. The bank still says 'no authority'. Tell me honestly, with an LPA would we really have skipped all this?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-28, C-21):</b> Honestly: yes for the paperwork, no for the grief. With a registered LPA naming you, her doctor certifies, you declare on OPGO and send the bank the LPA the same afternoon. No application, no affidavits, no waiting for a court. The same love, without the sixteen weeks.</p><p class=r><b>Carol replies:</b> 'The same love without the sixteen weeks.' I'm putting that on the poster.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> The court prefers to keep a deputy's powers as limited as reasonably practicable, and OPG supervises deputies afterwards.</p><p class=r><b>Carol replies:</b> More supervision. Wonderful.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> With an LPA you'd have full control of everything from day one, no questions asked.</p><p class=r><b>Carol replies:</b> Nothing in this building is 'no questions asked'.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Over-claim: donees act only when capacity is lacking, within best interests and any restrictions, s 13(1), (10)</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — Law-firm steps · Evening</h3><p><b>Carol:</b> Here's the real reason. Every day I decide for Mum: which home, which treatment to consent to, whether to lock the gate so she doesn't wander. I hate it. Why would I hand that weight to Marcus for me?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-13, C-08, C-36):</b> Because the law gives him a rulebook you never had for Mum. He must act in your best interests, consider your wishes and anything you wrote down, and he's protected if he acts in good faith. Write your wishes now: the home you'd accept, who to consult, what a good day looks like. That's not a burden. It's a map.</p><p class=r><b>Carol replies:</b> A map. Mum left me nothing but a photo album and a lot of guessing. …OK. Marcus, with Ryan as replacement. And I'm writing the map tonight.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> On the gate, restraint is only allowed if it's necessary to prevent harm and proportionate, and the less restrictive option should be considered, like a dementia day-care centre.</p><p class=r><b>Carol replies:</b> Day care's on the list. Still not about me, is it.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Don't overthink it, Marcus can decide anything at all for you, including stopping treatment if it gets bad.</p><p class=r><b>Carol replies:</b> He can't, actually, and I'd never ask him to carry that.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: donees cannot decide on life-sustaining treatment, s 13(8)</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Carol pins her name and, beside it, a photo of Mum with the caption "Do it while you can".</p><p><b>Legacy line (Carol):</b> Marcus is my donee. I wrote him four pages. Mum's hearing is in three weeks.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">10. Wheeling Wilson [wilson]</h2><p class=m>Wilson, 52 · IT support officer, Town Council</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>Signing this feels like admitting what everyone already assumes about me.</li><li>I already gave my ex-wife a letter of authorisation years ago.</li><li>Online forms and a doctor 'assessing' me, I've had enough people assessing me.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Town Council office · Morning</h3><p><b>Wilson:</b> Pass the flat-head. Lisa, people look at the chair and assume I can't decide what to have for lunch. You want me to sign a paper about not being able to decide? Hard no.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-01, C-03, C-31):</b> The paper says the opposite. The Act presumes you have capacity, and OPG's guide says flat out that a physical disability, how you look or how you behave can't be the basis for saying you can't decide. An LPA is you, at full capacity, choosing who speaks for you if a doctor ever certifies you can't.</p><p class=r><b>Wilson replies:</b> 'Physical disability can't be the basis.' That's in their own guide? …Screwdriver back. Come to the game tonight.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Capacity is decision-specific, you might lack it for one thing and not another.</p><p class=r><b>Wilson replies:</b> That's a very lawyerly way of saying 'maybe you can't'. Next.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Come on, at some point everyone in a chair will need someone to take over.</p><p class=r><b>Wilson replies:</b> Get out from under my desk.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Exactly the unjustified assumption s 4(3) forbids</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Sports Hall · Evening (half-time)</h3><p><b>Wilson:</b> I already sorted this. When Rachel was small I gave her mother a letter authorising her to handle my stuff if I was in hospital. Signed, dated. Done.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-27, C-19, C-06):</b> Two problems. That letter is an ordinary authorisation, and those end the moment you lose capacity, the only time you'd need it. And divorce ends a donee's appointment unless the LPA says otherwise. It dies exactly when it's needed. Rachel's 24 now; she can be your donee.</p><p class=r><b>Wilson replies:</b> Dies when it's needed. That's… actually funny. Rachel would kill me if she knew her mum still had that letter.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> You should tell OPG if your circumstances change, donors must inform the Public Guardian of certain events.</p><p class=r><b>Wilson replies:</b> Inform them about a letter they've never seen? Sure.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Just update the letter with Rachel's name and get it witnessed, same thing as an LPA.</p><p class=r><b>Wilson replies:</b> If a witnessed letter were the same thing, why does the OPG exist?</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: only an instrument in the prescribed form, certified and registered, creates an LPA, s 11(3)</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — MRT lift lobby · Night</h3><p><b>Wilson:</b> Last thing. 'Do it on the portal, see a doctor who assesses you.' I've done more forms and assessments than you've had birthdays. If the portal isn't accessible, or the doctor talks to my chair instead of me, I'm out.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-18, C-04, C-17):</b> Both are covered. The certificate issuer must explain things in a way that suits you, and you can communicate by any means. She isn't grading you, just confirming you understood and nobody pushed you; then you both sign in the Singpass app. Dr Cheryl is OPG-accredited, and I'll be in the room if you want a witness to the eye contact.</p><p class=r><b>Wilson replies:</b> A witness to the eye contact. Deal. Rachel as donee, my basketball captain as replacement. Book me before the lift comes back.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> The fee for Form 1 is waived for citizens, so at least that part's easy.</p><p class=r><b>Wilson replies:</b> I didn't ask about money.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Don't worry, the doctor's assessment is a formality, they never say no.</p><p class=r><b>Wilson replies:</b> Then what's the point of it? Either it means something or it doesn't.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Dismissive and untrue: the CI must genuinely certify understanding and absence of pressure</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Wilson installs a ramp at the CC side entrance and the Legacy Day booth gets an "accessible booth" sign.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Wilson):</b> Rachel's my donee. The doctor looked at me, not the chair. Small things.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">11. Teaching Thilaga [thilaga]</h2><p class=m>Thilaga, 56 · Senior Tamil-language teacher</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>The process is complicated and I don't trust doing legal things online.</li><li>What if I get something wrong? Can it be corrected? Can I change my mind?</li><li>Arvind is overseas, if I appoint him, is it useless?</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Primary 4 classroom · Morning</h3><p><b>Thilaga:</b> Lisa, stand by the door. An LPA is a legal deed. Legal deeds need lawyers, forms, stamps. I am not doing a deed on my handphone between marking and cooking.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-16, C-18):</b> Then do it like a lesson plan. Step one: Form 1 on OPGO, tick-boxes, the volunteers type. Step two: Kumar and Arvind accept on their phones with Singpass. Step three: Dr Cheryl sits with you, then you both sign in the Singpass app. Step four: a three-week waiting period, then registered, with an SMS at every stage. One hour, zero fee.</p><p class=r><b>Thilaga replies:</b> Four steps. SMS at every stage. …Write that down for me properly and I'll read it at the library.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> There's a study showing people who apply online often need help, which is why the roadshow exists.</p><p class=r><b>Thilaga replies:</b> So the study says it's hard. Thank you for confirming.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> It's so simple you don't even need to read the form, just tick everything.</p><p class=r><b>Thilaga replies:</b> 'Don't read the form.' I have taught children for thirty years and that is the worst thing I have ever heard.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Reckless: the ticked options change what the donee can do, and nothing can be amended after submission, QRG "Certify" step 4</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Classroom · Midday (marking)</h3><p><b>Thilaga:</b> Suppose I tick the wrong box. Suppose I change my mind about who. Is it carved in stone? Because 'registered by the government' sounds carved in stone.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-19, C-10, C-17):</b> The opposite of stone while you have capacity. Dr Cheryl checks every box with you before you sign, because nothing can be edited after submission. If you ever want a change, you revoke on OPGO and make a new one. And your donees can't act until you lack capacity, so a wrong box today changes nothing today.</p><p class=r><b>Thilaga replies:</b> Checked before. Revocable after. Dormant in between. Those are good words. …Come to the café at six; I'll have questions about my son.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> If a donee dies or goes bankrupt, their appointment ends, so it's worth naming a replacement.</p><p class=r><b>Thilaga replies:</b> I asked about me, not them.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Once it's registered, that's it, you can't change your donees without going to court.</p><p class=r><b>Thilaga replies:</b> Then I certainly will not sign anything.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: the donor can revoke while capable, s 15(2); Guide D5</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — CC café · Evening</h3><p><b>Thilaga:</b> Arvind is in Melbourne. If I name him, he can't stand in a bank in Toa Payoh. If I don't name him, he'll think I don't trust him. You see my problem.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-07):</b> Name both, Kumar and Arvind, jointly and severally: either can act, so Kumar handles the bank and Arvind is a full donee. Leave the form silent and the law assumes 'jointly', which makes Arvind's distance a problem. He has to accept on OPGO with his own Singpass before Dr Cheryl can certify, so he'll know.</p><p class=r><b>Thilaga replies:</b> Jointly and severally, not silent. Both names, either acts, and he accepts first. …Book us. Kumar too, he thinks his CPF nomination covers this, and I gather it does not.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Under the Study, almost everyone appoints family, sons are common donees.</p><p class=r><b>Thilaga replies:</b> I didn't ask what is common. I asked what works.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Overseas donees aren't allowed, so it has to be Kumar only.</p><p class=r><b>Thilaga replies:</b> Not allowed? Show me the section.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: False: no residency requirement in s 12; practicality is the issue, not eligibility</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> Thilaga becomes the roadshow's Tamil-language helper and hands Lisa a laminated "4 steps" card: 1 Draft Form 1 on OPGO · 2 Donees accept with Singpass · 3 CI certifies, both sign in Singpass app, CI submits · 4 Pay if required → 3-week wait → registered.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Thilaga):</b> Four steps. I've written it in Tamil and English for the auntie next door.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">12. Fishing Fauzi [fauzi]</h2><p class=m>Fauzi, 59 · Tailor, Ketapang Boutique</p><p><b>Doubts</b></p><ul><li>My children will decide for me, good kids, all of them.</li><li>If I choose one, the other three will be hurt.</li><li>I'll do it after I retire; I'm busy till then.</li></ul><h3>Encounter 1 — Kampung jetty · Evening</h3><p><b>Fauzi:</b> Lisa, sit. Four children, all good. Anything happens, they decide. Why do I need the government's form for my own children?</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-21, C-26):</b> The form is how the law learns they're your children with your say-so. Without it none of the four has authority; one would apply to court and the others would have to agree who. Half the country believes what you said, Uncle. It's the top reason families end up in front of a judge instead of at the bedside.</p><p class=r><b>Fauzi replies:</b> In front of a judge instead of the bedside. Hm. Fish is biting; come back tomorrow.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> Deputies appointed by the court aren't paid, and the court decides the scope of their powers.</p><p class=r><b>Fauzi replies:</b> So the court sorts it. Good. Fish.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> You're diabetic, Uncle, you could lose capacity any day.</p><p class=r><b>Fauzi replies:</b> My sugar is fine, thank you. Don't talk about me like a broken pipe.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Crude; a medical condition alone says nothing about capacity, s 4(3)</i></p><h3>Encounter 2 — Ketapang Boutique · Midday</h3><p><b>Fauzi:</b> Here's the real thing. I pick one, say Farah, the eldest. The other three: 'Abah doesn't trust us.' Ten years of Hari Raya spoiled. Not worth it.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-07, C-15, C-34, C-35):</b> Then don't pick one. Name Farah and Hakim jointly and severally, so either handles daily things; tick the Form 1 box so nobody deals with this house without the court; add a third child as replacement. Tell all four at dinner why. If donees disagree, OPG's guide sends them to mediation, not a judge. That's not distrust. That's the opposite.</p><p class=r><b>Fauzi replies:</b> Two who can act, court for the house, one in reserve, and if they quarrel, mediation. Like a shift roster with a supervisor. …Zainab will like that. She hates fights more than I do.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> A recent study found 'avoiding family disputes' is one of the main reasons people even appoint professionals.</p><p class=r><b>Fauzi replies:</b> Pay a stranger so my children don't fight? No.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Appoint all four jointly, then nobody can complain, everyone must agree.</p><p class=r><b>Fauzi replies:</b> All four agree on anything? You haven't met my children.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Form 1 takes at most two donees, and jointly means if they can't agree, none can act, s 12(4)–(6)</i></p><h3>Encounter 3 — Kampung chess table · Night</h3><p><b>Fauzi:</b> Alright. After I retire. Eleven months. Then I'll have time for forms.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option A (Convince; cards C-05, C-10, C-02):</b> Eleven months is a bet that nothing happens. You can only sign while you have capacity; a stroke tomorrow and it's the court route for Farah. Sunday is one hour, and the form sleeps until needed. Bring Zainab, hers matters just as much.</p><p class=r><b>Fauzi replies:</b> 'A bet that nothing happens.' Checkmate on me, boy. Sunday. Both of us.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option B (Neutral):</b> The study found people keep thinking the 'right age' is a bit older than they are now.</p><p class=r><b>Fauzi replies:</b> Ha! That's me exactly. Still. Eleven months.</p><p class=o><b>Lisa option C (Backfire):</b> Sign now or it might cost more later.</p><p class=r><b>Fauzi replies:</b> Now you're a salesman.</p><p class=r><i>Why it backfires: Fear-based and unfounded: the citizen fee is zero, permanently, since 1 April 2026</i></p><h3>Outcome</h3><p><b>Done:</b> The pail on the jetty gets a "Legacy Day" sticker; all four children appear at the CC noticeboard on Saturday for a family photo.</p><p><b>Legacy line (Fauzi):</b> Farah and Hakim, either one for daily; the court for the house; Amirah in reserve. Nobody sulked. Zainab did hers first, to shame me.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">Story events</h2><h3>Wednesday evening: Uncle Tan [tan]</h3><p>A non-target neighbour feeding pigeons at the plaza collapses. An ambulance takes him to Tenang General. Lisa follows and overhears the family being told the bank needs a court order, a deputyship, because there is no LPA.</p><p class=m>Card: C-21</p><h3>Friday night: Ah Gong's letter [ahgong]</h3><p>At the hospital, Lisa's mother shows him the deputyship court papers for Ah Gong. "We never knew what Pa wanted."</p><p class=m>Card: C-13</p><h3>Kampung noticeboard [poster]</h3><p>TENANG LEGACY DAY. Sunday 09:00, Tenang Community Club. OPG officers, two certificate issuers on site (an OPG-accredited GP and a practising lawyer), volunteers for the online application, free kopi. Bring your donee(s), or have them accept on OPGO first. Bring your phone with the Singpass app.</p><h2 style="page-break-before:always">Fact Bank</h2><p><b>C-01</b> You are presumed to have capacity until it is shown otherwise. Making an unwise decision doesn't mean you lack capacity.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 3(2), 3(4); Guide Part E (Principles 1 &amp; 3)</p><p><b>C-02</b> Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific: you can lack it for one matter and not another, or lose it and regain it. Temporary loss counts (a coma, delirium after surgery).</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 4(1)–(2); Guide A1</p><p><b>C-03</b> Nobody can decide you lack capacity just from your age, appearance (features of a condition, how you're dressed), a condition (a physical or intellectual disability, an age-related illness, even being drunk), or how you behave.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 4(3); DGuide A1</p><p><b>C-04</b> The test is four abilities: understand the information, retain it, use or weigh it, and communicate the decision, by talking, signing, blinking, any means. Retaining it only briefly is still enough. Explanations must be given in a way that suits you (simple words, large print, drawings, sign language).</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 5(1)–(3); Guide Part E (Principle 2)</p><p><b>C-05</b> To make an LPA you must be 21 or older and have capacity at the moment you sign. Once capacity is lost, it is too late to make one, the family must go to court instead.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 11(2)(c); Guide B; Brochure</p><p><b>C-06</b> A donee must be 21+. A personal-welfare donee must be a named individual, not "my lawyer", not a company. A property-and-affairs donee can be an individual who is not bankrupt, or a licensed trust company (property only). A lay donee cannot be paid. You yourself cannot appoint a property-and-affairs donee while you are bankrupt. The certificate issuer cannot be your family member or your donee.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 12(1)–(2); Regs reg 7(2); Guide B1, D1; DGuide C2; QRG Form 1 step 8</p><p><b>C-07</b> With two or more donees you choose: jointly (all must agree, if they can't, they can't act on that decision), or jointly and severally (any one can act). If you don't specify, the law assumes jointly. Form 1 takes up to 2 donees and 1 replacement donee, and you pick the manner once for personal welfare and once for property. A replacement donee steps in if a donee disclaims, dies, goes bankrupt (property part), divorces you, or loses capacity, and you can say which donee they replace, for which powers.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 12(4)–(9); Guide B2–B3; QRG Form 1 steps 10–18</p><p><b>C-08</b> Personal-welfare donees can decide where you live and with whom, day-to-day care (what you eat, wear, do), your social activities, your correspondence and who you have contact with. Whether they can consent to or refuse your medical treatment (including clinical trials) is a Yes/No question you answer in Form 1.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 13(6)–(7); Guide B4; QRG Form 1 step 13</p><p><b>C-09</b> Property-and-affairs donees can operate your bank accounts, pay rent, bills and the mortgage, collect income, dividends and insurance payouts, handle tax and CPF matters, invest savings, buy or sell property, and buy equipment you need.</p><p class=m>Source: Guide B4; DGuide C7</p><p><b>C-10</b> The LPA sleeps until it is needed. Your donee may only act when you lack capacity (or reasonably believes you do), and must step aside again if you regain it. While you can decide, you decide.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 13(1); Guide B5; DGuide D1–D2</p><p><b>C-11</b> A donee can never decide on life-sustaining treatment, or on treatment needed to prevent serious deterioration, doctors decide those in your best interests, and if you made an Advance Medical Directive the doctors must follow it. A donee also cannot make a will for you, or make/revoke CPF or insurance nominations.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 13(8), 13(9), 13(9A); Guide B5; DGuide C3, C4, C8</p><p><b>C-12</b> Some decisions nobody can make for you: marriage, sex, divorce on three years' separation, adoption, changing religion, gender-change treatment, sterilisation, abortion, organ-donation objections, an Advance Medical Directive, gifting your body, and nobody can vote for you.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 26, s 28; Guide B6; DGuide C5</p><p><b>C-13</b> "Best interests" is a legal duty: consider everything relevant, whether you'll regain capacity, involve you as much as possible, your past and present wishes (especially anything you wrote down while you had capacity), your beliefs and values, and consult the people you named and those who care for you. Your donee should also consider any Advance Care Planning you did.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 6(2)–(9); Guide Part E (Principle 4); DGuide D3</p><p><b>C-14</b> A donee can only give gifts from your money if the LPA expressly allows it. Form 1 asks you whether gifts are allowed and lets you state the value. If you allow gifts without fixing a value, they must be reasonable for the size of your estate, and your property must be preserved for your own maintenance first.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 14, s 6(7); DGuide C8 [VERIFY exact Form 1 options]</p><p><b>C-15</b> In Form 1 you can require your donee to get the court's approval before selling, transferring, mortgaging or otherwise dealing with a property, you type the property's address into the form.</p><p class=m>Source: QRG Form 1 step 14</p><p><b>C-16</b> Form 1 is the standard form, mostly tick-boxes granting wide powers, with basic conditions; you can complete it yourself (consult a lawyer if unsure what "wide powers" means). Form 2 has free-text spaces for specific or customised powers and must be drafted by a lawyer, who can also be your certificate issuer. Both cover personal welfare, property and affairs, or both.</p><p class=m>Source: Guide B; DGuide A4; QRG "Make an LPA (Form 2)"</p><p><b>C-17</b> A certificate issuer, an OPG-accredited doctor, a practising lawyer, or a psychiatrist, meets you in person after all your donees have accepted, and checks that you understand the purpose and scope of the LPA, that there is no fraud or pressure, and nothing else stops the LPA being made. You both sign digitally with the Singpass app; the CI then submits it to OPG immediately. CIs charge their own professional fee; some not-for-profits certify at affordable rates.</p><p class=m>Source: Regs reg 7(1); Guide D1 step 2; QRG "Certify an LPA"</p><p><b>C-18</b> LPAs are made on OPGO with Singpass, in this order: draft Form 1 → each donee and replacement donee accepts online (they get an SMS/email) → CI certifies and submits → pay if required → mandatory 3-week waiting period for objections → registered (about 8 working days on top of the 3 weeks; you get SMS updates at every stage). Since 1 April 2026 the Form 1 application fee is permanently waived for Singapore citizens; PRs, foreigners and Form 2 still pay, and the CI's fee always applies. If a donee rejects, the draft is void and you start again. Knowingly making a false statement in the application is a crime (fine up to $10,000 or 2 years' jail).</p><p class=m>Source: Guide D1–D2, D6, Part F; QRG steps 19–29; Brochure; Parliament 5 Mar 2026 [VERIFY fee table]</p><p><b>C-19</b> While you have capacity you can revoke your LPA any time: tell every donee in writing first, then file the notice on OPGO (a revocation fee applies); you can start drafting a new LPA while the old one is being cancelled. Nothing can be amended after submission, changing a donee means revoke-and-remake. Your bankruptcy ends the property part. A donee's appointment ends on death, bankruptcy (property part), divorce or annulment from you (unless your LPA says otherwise), formal disclaimer, or loss of capacity, and if joint donees lose one, the whole LPA fails unless a replacement or a joint-and-several donee remains.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 15; Guide B4, D5; DGuide C6, C9; QRG "Revoke LPA"</p><p><b>C-20</b> Safeguards: the Public Guardian keeps the register, receives reports, handles complaints about donees, investigates breaches, and can send a Board of Visitors member (a medically qualified Special Visitor or a General Visitor) to visit you or your donee. Since 2016 the court can suspend a donee's powers immediately if there is a risk your assets will be dissipated, and can revoke the LPA.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 31, s 35, s 36A; Guide A3</p><p><b>C-21</b> Without an LPA, your spouse and children have no automatic legal authority over your money or your care decisions. Someone must apply to the court to be your deputy: time, cost, uncertainty, and the court decides how much power to give. Deputies are unpaid unless professional, are supervised by OPG, and can't be given power to make gifts or your will.</p><p class=m>Source: Study 2026 p 30–32; MCA s 25(3); Brochure "True or False?"</p><p><b>C-22</b> If you have no one suitable to appoint, you can appoint a Professional Donee registered with OPG, lawyers, doctors, accountants, nurses, allied health and social work professionals who meet OPG's criteria and pass a certification course, or, for property, a licensed trust company. You agree the fee with them upfront; they must file an annual report to OPG and stay answerable to their own professional body. About 1 in 10 people intending to make an LPA now plan to.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 12(1)(a)(ii)–(iii); Guide C1–C4; Brochure; Study 2026 Fig. 9–10</p><p><b>C-23</b> Even without an LPA, doctors and carers can give care or treatment that is in your best interests if they reasonably believe you lack capacity, but they cannot act against a decision your donee made, and restraint is only allowed if necessary and proportionate.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 7, s 8; Guide A2(d); DGuide C4</p><p><b>C-24</b> Parents of a young person with an intellectual disability lose their legal say when the child turns 21. The court can appoint them deputies before 21 if the child is likely to still lack capacity; parents are preferred; another person can be named Successor Deputy. Cash assets can go into a Special Needs Trust.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 21; Guide A2(c)</p><p><b>C-25</b> An LPA is not a will (a will only speaks at death and has its own capacity test, the LPA is revoked at death and the will then takes over), not an Advance Medical Directive (a separate Act, and a donee can't make one for you), and not Advance Care Planning (non-binding wishes your donee should still consider). Do them together; they answer different questions.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 26(j); Wills Act; Brochure; DGuide D3</p><p><b>C-26</b> The numbers: about 400,000 Singapore citizens, roughly one in seven, had an LPA by early 2026. Among 49–80-year-olds, 7 in 10 have heard of it, 31% have one, 41% neither have nor intend to. Top reason for not doing it: "my children will automatically have the right" (50.8%). One in four Singaporeans will be 65+ by 2030.</p><p class=m>Source: Study 2026 pp 3, 5, 23, 30 [VERIFY]</p><p><b>C-27</b> An ordinary power of attorney ends when you lose capacity. The LPA is the one designed to last through it. That's the "L".</p><p class=m>Source: Guide B</p><p><b>C-28</b> Activating the LPA: when you lose capacity, a doctor certifies it; your donee logs into OPGO, declares that a medical practitioner has certified you lack capacity, and sends the password-protected registered LPA to the bank, hospital or agency from OPGO. Each institution may have its own extra requirements, banks follow the Association of Banks' guidelines (Aug 2026), and may ask for a doctor's certificate that your incapacity is likely to be permanent.</p><p class=m>Source: Guide D1 step 3, D3; DGuide A4; QRG "View and Send LPA"; MCA s 13(10) [VERIFY ABS guidelines]</p><p><b>C-29</b> Donees are accountable: they must keep records (and accounts, for property), keep your money separate from theirs, act only within the LPA, and cannot hand their authority to anyone else. The Public Guardian can require reports or information and investigate complaints; the court can call for a Board of Visitors report.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 31(1)(f); Regs regs 31–33, 38; DGuide C3, C8</p><p><b>C-30</b> Singapore's law expects family to be the first line of support, but "family first" only works if the law knows which family member you chose. Singles and childless couples increasingly need friends or professionals as donees.</p><p class=m>Source: Study 2026 p 31; Guide C1</p><p><b>C-31</b> Five statutory principles everyone acting for you must follow: (1) presume capacity; (2) give all practicable help before concluding someone can't decide; (3) an unwise decision is not incapacity; (4) act in the person's best interests; (5) choose the less restrictive option, sometimes that means doing nothing.</p><p class=m>Source: MCA s 3; Guide Part E; DGuide D4</p><p><b>C-32</b> A donee's dos and don'ts: follow the statutory principles and the Code of Practice; act in good faith, with reasonable care and skill; respect confidentiality; keep records (and accounts, for property); don't benefit yourself; don't pass your authority to anyone else; don't give up the role without telling the donor and OPG.</p><p class=m>Source: DGuide C3, C8</p><p><b>C-33</b> After your LPA is registered: you and your donees can view and download it on OPGO any time. Tell the people your donee will one day deal with, your doctor, your bank, the CPF Board, insurers, CDP or your broker, that you've made an LPA and ask what they'll need. Anyone with a good reason can search the register or request a certified copy for a small fee.</p><p class=m>Source: Guide D3; QRG "View and Send", "Search Request", "CTC Application"</p><p><b>C-34</b> If your donees disagree with each other, or with a doctor: hold a best-interests meeting; get a second opinion; try mediation (Community Mediation Centre, Singapore Mediation Centre); ask a Family Service Centre for help. Court is the last resort.</p><p class=m>Source: DGuide Part E (Halimah's story)</p><p><b>C-35</b> Form 1 limits: up to 2 donees + 1 replacement donee; one manner of acting for personal welfare and one for property; a trust company can be a property donee only; a replacement can be assigned to a specific donee and specific powers. Anything finer, more donees, split rules within property, standing instructions, is Form 2.</p><p class=m>Source: QRG Form 1 steps 10–18, Form 2 step 10; Guide B2</p><p><b>C-36</b> Your donee is protected too: a donee who acts in good faith under the LPA is not liable just because it later turns out the power didn't exist or had ended, unless they knew. Being asked is not being set up.</p><p class=m>Source: DGuide C12; MCA s 13</p></body></html>